      "seed": 387136343,
      "version": 2066,
      "versionNonce": 2113343993,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1752904733387,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How is \"budget\" defined? I was originally thinking that a budget would\njust be totalIncome - allExpenses. But for an advanced app, more\npeople define budget as a particular amount of money you allot\nyourself for a specific thing/category.\n\nIn this case, how would a budget be different from a category?\n\nThe purpose of a category is to group expenses together.\nA budget would then take that category, total it, then subtract it\nfrom the income.\n\n- It would dynamic. You have the total monthly/biweekly income which\nis how much you can spend.\n\n- Then, you can have some \"business\" logic to add up all the budgets\nand see:\n\nif ( biweeklyBudgetsTotal > biweeklyIncome ) {\n        display/print \"ERROR! - You have gone over your! {Biweekly}\nBudget Exceeds {Biweekly} Income!\n}\n\nOR! I could just have a plannedAmount field and an actualSpent field\nand compare those two values.\n\nAllow users to create their own categories that they then can attach\nexpenses to.",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "vkvXEOFcminU6E_3Le1jP",
      "originalText": "How is \"budget\" defined? I was originally thinking that a budget would just be totalIncome - allExpenses. But for an advanced app, more people define budget as a particular amount of money you allot yourself for a specific thing/category.\n\nIn this case, how would a budget be different from a category?\n\nThe purpose of a category is to group expenses together.\nA budget would then take that category, total it, then subtract it from the income.\n\n- It would dynamic. You have the total monthly/biweekly income which is how much you can spend.\n\n- Then, you can have some \"business\" logic to add up all the budgets and see:\n\nif ( biweeklyBudgetsTotal > biweeklyIncome ) {\n        display/print \"ERROR! - You have gone over your! {Biweekly} Budget Exceeds {Biweekly} Income!\n}\n\nOR! I could just have a plannedAmount field and an actualSpent field and compare those two values.\n\nAllow users to create their own categories that they then can attach expenses to.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    }
  ],
